Q: JavaScript - Why this multiplier closure function works? A: <p><code>multiplier</code> is a function which returns another, capturing the <code>factor</code> reference within the nested function.</p>
<pre><code>function multiplier(factor) {
  return number =&gt; number * factor;
}
</code></pre>
<p>So, when you call <code>mulitplier(2)</code> you get a reference to (roughly) this function:</p>
<pre><code>function(number) {
  return number * 2; // 2 comes from the factor argument in the parent context
}
</code></pre>
<p>When you call this function, which you've aliased <code>twice()</code>,  you're just multiplying the input <code>number</code> by <code>2</code>. The point is that the function returned from <code>multiplier()</code> behaves differently based on the <code>factor</code> you provide, e.g.:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function multiplier(factor) {
  return number =&gt; number * factor;
}

const twice = multiplier(2);
const triple = multiplier(3);
const quadruple = multiplier(4);

console.log(twice(5));
console.log(triple(5));
console.log(quadruple(5));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>You can achieve a similar result with <code>Function.prototype.<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Function/bind" rel="nofollow noreferrer">bind</a></code>, which allows you to set your <code>this</code> context as well as arguments, e.g.:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function multiply(factor, number) {
  return factor * number;
}

const twice = multiply.bind(null, 2);
const triple = multiply.bind(null, 3);
const quadruple = multiply.bind(null, 4);

console.log(twice(5));
console.log(triple(5));
console.log(quadruple(5));</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
